 ],
 "literal": "Do not come to our street, O garlanded one who stood there! / She is becoming the subject of gossip, O Lord, like Kaveri's / great bathing ghat where many bathe, tied to the marutam trees, / with lofted-tusked elephants, of Cēntaṉ's father, / who has toddy feasts and packs of hunting dogs, / the great leader of youths with rows of bright swords, / Aḻici's town Ārkkāṭu, this woman, / seeing her blameless great beauty ruining.",
 "translation": "Do not come to our street, O garlanded one, standing there! Gossip is arising, O Lord, about this girl. Seeing her blameless, great beauty fade away, people talk of her as they would of Ārkkāṭu, the town of Aḻici—the great leader of young warriors with rows of bright swords, who hunt with packs of dogs and feast on toddy, and the father of Cēntaṉ, who owns elephants with lofted tusks that are tied to the marutam trees at the great Kaveri ghat where many bathe.",
 "modern": "மாலை அணிந்தவரே, அங்கே நிற்காதீர், எமது தெருவுக்கு வராதீர்! பலரும் நீராடும் காவிரியின் பெரிய துறையிலுள்ள மருத மரங்களில் கட்டப்பட்ட, உயர்ந்த தந்தங்களை உடைய யானைகளைக் கொண்ட சேந்தனின் தந்தையும், கள் விருந்தும் வேட்டை நாய் கூட்டமும் உடையவனும், வரிசையான ஒளிரும் வாள்களை ஏந்திய இளைஞர்களின் தலைவனுமாகிய அழிசியின் ஆர்க்காடு என்னும் ஊரைப் போல, இவளுடைய பழி அற்ற சிறந்த அழகு அழிவதைக் கண்டு, பெருமானே, இவளைப் பற்றி ஊரார் அலர் தூற்றுகின்றனர்.",
 "explanation": "The friend of the heroine is speaking to the hero who has returned from his trysts with courtesans. The hero has been unfaithful, and his visits to the heroine's street are causing public gossip. The friend tells him not to stand in their street. The poem uses a rich description of the town of Ārkkāṭu, ruled by Aḻici (father of Cēntaṉ). The image of elephants with lofted tusks tied to the marutam trees at the busy Kaveri bathing ghat represents how the heroine's beauty is bound and exposed to public gaze and gossip, just like the elephants at the busy ghat. Key words include 'அலராகின்றாள்' (she becomes gossip), 'மாணலம் தொலைதல்' (ruining of great beauty), and 'வாரல் எம் சேரி' (do not come to our street).",
